const w = WEAPONS[i++];
while (group.children.length) group.remove(group.children[0]);
const model = buildWeaponModel(w);
...
group.add(model);
group.rotation.set(0, .16, 0);
group.position.set(0, 0, 0);
group.updateMatrixWorld(true);
const bbox = new THREE.Box3().setFromObject(model);
```

Could `Box3.setFromObject` use the *parent's* matrixWorld? It expands by each mesh's geometry transformed by mesh.matrixWorld, which includes group transform. That's fine.

Hmm, but at odd iterations, the group scale from the previous iteration is still set! We set `group.scale.setScalar(s)` at the end of each iteration. At the start of the next iteration we DON'T reset group.scale to 1 before computing bbox! That's it. The bbox computed includes the leftover scale from the previous weapon (e.g., s=6.72), so the deagle's 0.52*6.72 = 3.49 — exactly! Deagle z=0.52 * 6.72 = 3.49. Yes! And x=0.08*6.72=0.54, but reported 0.97 due to rotation maybe. Close enough. Next iteration raygun (normal because previous scale 0.86 applied? raygun 0.53*0.86=0.46, reported 0.53, odd... maybe some resetting by rotation).

So the bug: group.scale persists between iterations, compounding. Fix: reset `group.scale.setScalar(1)` and rotation/position at the start before bbox. Then all weapons rendered at correct fit size. Great.

Also height/width offsets: after scaling with model.position=-center, the offset issue remains. To center properly, we should center the model by putting it in a pivot group? Better: compute bbox at scale 1, then set `model.position.sub(center)` and then scale the model? If we scale group, the position offset scales. Use a wrapper:
```
const pivot = new THREE.Group(); pivot.add(model); group.add(pivot)
model.position.sub(center)
group.scale.setScalar(s)
```
Still same. To keep centered: set model.position = -center, then group.scale scales position → offset. Instead scale model itself: `model.scale.setScalar(s)` then `model.position.copy(center).multiplyScalar(-s)`? Since model's own position is scaled by its own scale? In Three, an object's local matrix = T * R * S, so position is not scaled by its own scale (position is translation added after scale). Actually matrix = T * R * S. So if model.scale = s and model.position = -center*s, then geometry point p → s*(p - center)... Let's verify: world = T(-center*s?) no. For object matrix = T(position) * R * S(scale): point p → position + scale*p (ignoring rotation). So to center bbox at 0: position = -scale*center. So set model.scale.setScalar(s) and model.position.copy(center).multiplyScalar(-s). But rotation also matters (R applied). With group rotation, we can leave model rotation identity. Simpler: keep group scale but set model position = -center and additionally group position = center*s? Because world = groupscale*(modelpos + p). To center bbox center: group.scale*modelpos + group.scale*center = 0 → group.scale*modelpos = -group.scale*center → modelpos = -center. Wait, that's what we have, so center should be zero! Let's recompute: point p, model position -center → in group space p - center; bbox center = center - center = 0. Group scale s → s*0 = 0. So it IS centered. I was wrong: scaling doesn't offset because position is part of the same transform. Right, group.matrix = T(0) R S; child at -center; total = S*(-center + p) = s*(p-center). Bbox center = 0. Good.

So only the leftover group.scale bug. Fix by resetting at start of each iteration:
```
group.scale.setScalar(1);
group.position.set(0,0,0);
group.rotation.set(0,0,0);
```
before building/bbox. Then after bbox, apply rotation.

This will make all guns render large and readable. Excellent.

